Equity Market Intelligence Platform — Project Summary
 
This project works through 1.01 million rows of daily OHLCV data across 455 NIFTY-listed
Indian stocks from 2014 to 2024. The data sits in a SQLite database alongside sector metadata,
India VIX, and US Fed Funds Rate from FRED. Ten-plus SQL queries cover the analytical
groundwork — sector CAGR, volatility ranking, drawdown depth, 52-week proximity, liquidity
screening, and sector rotation by calendar year.
 
The EDA starts with what the data actually looks like. NIFTY 50 returns show fat tails and
negative skew — visible in the histogram and QQ-plot. Rolling correlations between Financial Services and IT, and between
Healthcare and Automobiles, confirm the familiar pattern: they converge toward 1.0 during stress,
when diversification is supposed to hold. A volatility regime chart breaks the decade into low,
medium, and high periods. Drawdown analysis records the five worst peak-to-trough episodes with
dates and durations. The VaR backtest runs a trailing 252-day historical VaR at 95% and measures
how often the actual loss exceeded it.
 
ML Models: The feature matrix uses momentum, rolling volatility, RSI, MACD, Bollinger Band
position, volume ratio, India VIX, and sector dummies — built without lookahead, with a hard
2022 cutoff for train-test separation. 

Model A (Return Direction) runs Logistic Regression,
Random Forest, and XGBoost to classify next-month return direction. All three land near AUC
0.53–0.56 — consistent with weak-form efficiency. SHAP shows trailing volatility and
medium-horizon momentum carry the most signal.

Model B (Volatility Forecasting) uses XGBoost
Regression against a naive persistence benchmark; VIX level and trailing volatility dominate,
confirming volatility clustering. Mincer-Zarnowitz regression checks forecast calibration. 

Model C
(Portfolio Optimization) builds an efficient frontier on a ten-stock large-cap universe via SciPy,
then backtests equal-weight, minimum-variance, and maximum-Sharpe strategies against NIFTY 50
from 2022 to 2024, reporting Sharpe ratio, max drawdown, and Calmar ratio across all four series.

All Charts Produced
 
 1. NIFTY 50 daily log returns vs. fitted normal distribution (2014–2024)
 2. QQ-plot: NIFTY 50 daily returns vs. normal distribution
 3. Distribution of daily returns by sector — box plot (2014–2024)
 4. Monthly return correlation heatmap: top 20 large-cap stocks, hierarchically clustered
 5. 90-day rolling correlation: Financial Services vs Information Technology
 6. 90-day rolling correlation: Healthcare vs Automobile and Auto Components
 7. NIFTY 50 21-day rolling annualized volatility with regime bands (2014–2024)
 8. NIFTY 50 underwater chart: drawdown from running peak (2014–2024)
 9. VaR backtest: actual daily returns, rolling 95% VaR line, breach days highlighted
10. NIFTY 50 cumulative return vs. India VIX with shaded crisis periods (2014–2024)
11. Average monthly NIFTY 50 return by US Fed policy regime
12. ROC curve comparison: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost — Model A
13. SHAP summary plot: global feature importance for XGBoost return direction — Model A
14. Predicted vs. actual volatility scatter, all three models with 45-degree line — Model B
15. XGBoost predicted vs. actual volatility over time, cross-sectional average — Model B
16. Efficient frontier: top-10 large-cap universe with individual stock risk-return points — Model C
17. Cumulative returns: equal-weight, min-variance, max-Sharpe vs. NIFTY 50 (2022–2024) — Model C
 
The chatbot runs Qwen 2.5-1.5B-Instruct locally, translates natural-language questions into
SQLite queries, executes them against a read-only database, and returns a plain-English
interpretation via a Streamlit frontend.
